# Claude Code workflow reference

Env-specific facts and decisions only, each at its current state with a dated
annotation. Generic harness behavior is injected into every session or
answerable live via the claude-code-guide agent; measurements, research and
audit baselines live in the chezmoi repo's `docs/agents-audit-log.md`. If
behavior seems changed, verify with that agent and update this file.

## Models (last verified 2026-09-15)

- Global model is pinned in settings (`model` key) from the chezmoi repo as
  `{{ .agents.claude.defaults.model }}`; `/model` changes only the current
  session. The default is the frontier driver; `[1m]` selects its 1M context.
  The installed CLI is latest 2.1.272, which can serve Fable 5.1; changing
  channel does not update the source model pin. Driver effort stays the default.
- Pins use explicit model IDs, never bare aliases: aliases re-resolve across CLI releases.
- Specialist tiers are pinned in `.chezmoidata/agents.yaml` and do not follow
  `/model`. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL` is configured as the fallback for
  unpinned children at the top worker tier (`{{ .subagent_tiers.claude.top }}`),
  but definitions and built-ins may pin themselves; `claude-code-guide` uses
  Haiku. Forks (`subagent_type: fork`) run the parent model — a driver-context
  continuation, not a worker.
- Since Claude Code 2.1.251, subagent resolution is per-invocation `model`, then
  definition frontmatter (`inherit` = main conversation), then
  `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL`, then the main conversation (release note and
  sub-agents doc, 2026-09-15). Before 2.1.251 the env setting overrode the first
  two. No roster entry uses `inherit`; leave the `_FORCE` variant unset because
  it would erase specialist pins.
- The no-frontier-child policy has a partial mechanical guard:
  `~/.claude/hooks/deny-frontier-child.mjs` denies visible per-call requests for
  the frontier pin, `fable`, or `inherit`. It cannot see a third-party agent
  definition that declares `model: inherit` without a per-call model; the
  managed roster carries none. Defaults and hooks do not prove actual routing.
- After each CLI update, dispatch one unpinned child and one with an explicit
  `{{ .subagent_tiers.claude.top }}` override and verify `message.model` rather
  than self-report. Resume and upstream routing failures remain outside the
  hook; issue history and the latest sample are in the audit log.
- Every custom subagent loads `~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` (built-in Explore and Plan
  do not), so shared driver-only rules are grouped under "session driver".
  Continue an existing agent for follow-ups rather than re-spawning it; a
  long-lived subagent retains context (Fable 5 guide).
- Max has a rolling 5-hour window and shared weekly pool; Fable can consume up
  to 50% of weekly usage and uses limits faster than other models, but exact
  model and cache-read weighting is unpublished. Raw transcript token counts
  are not Max quota costs; reconcile the next binding limit against `/usage`.
- Subagents draw the same Max pools. Delegation is for bounded ownership and
  context quality, not assumed quota savings; neither the savings nor a single
  primary cost lever is verified.
- Codex pin caveat: openai/codex#32587 (open 2026-09-15) reports spawned
  children inheriting parent model/effort; verify via `/status` after updates.
- `codex-review` / `codex-advisor` are read-only Codex consultations for
  high-risk diffs or stuck calls; decision ownership stays in Claude. The
  bridge is configured for `{{ .subagent_tiers.codex.top }}` (`--model`,
  anchored in agents.yaml), but configured routing is not verified service.
- The codex-review trigger is risk-or-breadth, never blanket (decision
  2026-09-03); the audit log tracks findings the session actually fixed.

## Local-only surface (decision 2026-09-21)

- The CLI carries no claude.ai-product surface: account skill and plugin sync,
  claude.ai connectors and Remote Control are off in settings; the cron
  scheduler is disabled by env;
  `{{ .agents.claude.denied_tools | join "`, `" }}` are bare-denied, which
  removes them from the tool list. Bundled skills hidden via `skillOverrides` and the
  deny list live in agents.yaml (`agents.claude.skill_overrides`,
  `denied_tools`). Web search, Exa, context7 and Playwright stay — research
  subagents depend on them.
- Plugin skills ignore `skillOverrides` (`enabledPlugins` owns them), and
  `disableBundledSkills` is unused because it also drops the review skills.
  `/code-review ultra` and `claude --cloud` have no disable knob; both are
  user-invoked only.

## Codex transport: owned MCP bridge (decision 2026-09-05)

- `codex mcp-server` was deprecated in codex-cli 0.149.1, so the `codex` MCP
  entry spawns `scripts/codex-bridge.mjs` from the chezmoi source repo,
  wrapping the stable `codex exec` / `codex exec review` CLI. Tools: `review`,
  `advise`, `reply`; invocation fixed to read-only sandbox, never approvals,
  high reasoning, top worker tier — callers pick scope only. `review` states
  the scope inside the prompt when custom instructions are given: the CLI
  rejects a PROMPT combined with `--base`/`--uncommitted`.
- MCP transport kept deliberately: the bridge is harness-spawned outside the
  Bash sandbox (the `~/.codex` deny stays intact) and its rendered allow rule
  (`mcp__codex`) makes the tools prompt-free in plan mode (verified
  2026-09-05). Plan mode blocks MCP tools unless allow-listed (`readOnlyHint`
  ignored; upstream #12368 closed "not planned") and Bash has no plan-mode
  exemption, which rules out every Bash-based transport.

## Codex plugin: not installed (decision 2026-09-05)

- OpenAI's official plugin (`openai/codex-plugin-cc`, dissected at v1.0.6)
  stays uninstalled: it spawns the experimental `codex app-server` through
  sandboxed Bash — blocked by the `~/.codex` deny, never prompt-free in plan
  mode, on a protocol documented as changeable without notice. Reconsider only
  if `codex exec` itself breaks AND the sandbox/plan-mode constraints change.

## pi trial vs codex (decision 2026-09-05)

- pi (`earendil-works/pi`, adopted at v0.85.0) runs interactive GPT sessions
  alongside codex until one wins the trial; ChatGPT-subscription OAuth in
  third-party OSS harnesses is OpenAI-sanctioned (last verified 2026-09-05)
  and draws the same plan quota as codex.
- pi ships no permission system: the managed workflow extension leases a
  per-tool srt (`@anthropic-ai/sandbox-runtime`) worker for every
  `workspace_*` call with a profile rendered from agents.yaml, and its plan
  mode gives read-only planning (verified 2026-09-09).
- Trial decision trigger: revisit at the next `agent-instructions-audit`, or
  sooner once one harness clearly owns all interactive GPT sessions — then
  demote or drop the loser (codex keeps the review-backend role). Not
  decidable as of 2026-09-15 (status history in the audit log).
